<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Segestes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Segestes was a nobleman of the Cherusci in the early 1st century CE and a leading supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He warned the governor Varus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>' plans before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> in 9 CE, but was ignored. His daughter Thusnelda married Arminius against his will; in 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> relieved Segestes from a siege by Arminius, and Thusnelda was taken to Rome as a captive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/segestes.jpg" alt="Segestes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin West · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_West_(1738-1820)_-_The_Wife_of_Arminius_brought_captive_to_Germanicus_-_RCIN_405683_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Segestes belonged to the Cherusci, a Germanic people caught up in the Roman attempts to conquer northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under Augustus and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. His name is believed to come from Germanic roots meaning master of victory, *segaz, victory, and the Old Frankish gastes, master. He favoured Roman overlordship, and the marriage of his daughter Thusnelda to the Cheruscan noble and war leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>, made against his will, left him with a lasting grudge against his son-in-law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His warning to the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a> was not believed, and Varus and his three legions perished in the three-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a>, ambushed by several allied Germanic tribes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>. Segestes turned openly against Arminius when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 15 to renew the attempt at Roman rule. Besieged in his stronghold by his own countrymen, he appealed to Germanicus, whose forces relieved the siege, and he then handed over his pregnant daughter to Germanicus as a prisoner.</p><p>Thusnelda was displayed with her brother Segimundus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>' victory parade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 17, with her father watching as an honoured spectator, and she never returned to her homeland. Her son Thumelicus, born in captivity, was trained as a gladiator at Ravenna and is thought to have died in a fight before the age of 20. In 21 Segestes and other members of his family killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>. Germanicus gave Segestes a residence in a Roman province west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Segestes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cheruscan society was led by noble families competing for power and Roman favour; Arminius' rival <b>Segestes</b> stayed loyal to Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/cherusci">Cherusci</a></li></ul></section>
